  "text_input": "Patient is a 63-year-old female with a history of systemic hypertension, carcinoma of the sigmoid colon (on chemotherapy), and a previous right anterior communicating artery aneurysm clipping. She presented with a sudden onset of involuntary movements in all four limbs, frothing from the mouth, and up-rolling of eyes, lasting for a brief period without post-ictal confusion or incontinence. On examination, she was conscious and oriented, with a blood pressure of 100/70 mmHg and a pulse of 102 bpm. Investigations revealed neutrophilic leukocytosis, positive CRP, and liver metastasis on USG. Echo showed an EF of 55% with LV diastolic dysfunction. She was treated with IV antibiotics (Tazocet), anti-epileptics (Levipill), and analgesics. She was advised to follow up with a neurologist for further evaluation of the seizure disorder.",
